<Project>

  <!--
  ============================================================================
  SSP: solution-wide NuGet restore policy
  ============================================================================

  Imported by every project in the repository before its own PropertyGroup
  elements (the Directory.Build.props convention), so one place governs restore
  behaviour for src/, tests/ and the vendored _reference/ tree. Nothing here
  changes how C# code is compiled: each project keeps its own
  TreatWarningsAsErrors, Nullable and analyzer settings.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  ====================
  Since the .NET 8 SDK, NuGet performs a package vulnerability audit during
  restore (NuGetAudit, on by default). The audit downloads an advisory feed
  from every configured source. When that feed cannot be reached (offline
  workstation, air-gapped or proxy filtered CI that can serve the packages
  themselves but not the advisory index) the failure is reported as:

      NU1900: Error occurred while getting package vulnerability data:
              Unable to load the service index for source
              https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json.

  NU1900 says nothing about the dependencies. The package graph resolves
  correctly and restore would succeed on its own; the diagnostic describes an
  optional enhancement. But it is still emitted at warning level, and
  TreatWarningsAsErrors=true (src/SSP.Activation and
  tests/SSP.Activation.Tests) escalates every warning, including this network
  artifact, into an error. That fails "dotnet restore", "dotnet build" and
  "dotnet test" although the repository contains no defect.

  The escalation is by design and is not a bug to work around in code:
  NuGet.targets passes TreatWarningsAsErrors, WarningsAsErrors,
  WarningsNotAsErrors and NoWarn into the restore task, so the same four
  properties that govern the compiler also govern NU19xx restore diagnostics,
  and NoWarn is evaluated before the escalation (suppressing a code wins over
  promoting one).

  WHAT THIS FILE DOES
  ===================
  * NU1900 is removed from restore output. "The advisory feed was unreachable"
    is not actionable in source code, so it must not be a build failure and it
    is not worth a warning line on every restore either.
  * NU1901 to NU1904 (real advisories: low, moderate, high and critical severity
    against a direct dependency) are still printed, but are exempted from the
    warnings as errors escalation, so a disclosure published after the fact
    against, say, a test stack package cannot break every build of the product.
  * The audit stays ENABLED at its default breadth (NuGetAuditMode=direct for
    net8.0 targets) and default severity floor (NuGetAuditLevel=low): whenever
    the feed is reachable, vulnerable packages are reported exactly as before,
    and the vulnerable package report of the dotnet CLI keeps working.

  A CI job with a guaranteed feed that wants advisories to be fatal can
  re-enable the hard gate per invocation with /p:WarningsNotAsErrors=, and a
  job that wants no audit traffic at all can use /p:NuGetAudit=false.
  See BUILD.md for the offline build and test workflow.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1900</NoWarn>
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
  ============================================================================
  SSP: SSP_SKIP_EMBED normalisation
  ============================================================================

  SSP_SKIP_EMBED is the switch src/SSP.Server/SSP.Server.csproj uses to embed
  the framework dependent SSP.Client.dll instead of re publishing SSP.Client and
  SSP.ServiceHost as self contained single-file win-x64 images. Those two nested
  publishes are the only steps of a normal build that require a package feed
  beyond the projects' own dependencies (the win-x64 runtime packs), so this
  switch is the supported way to build and test offline.

  The repository documented both spellings of the switch (SSP_SKIP_EMBED=1 in
  prose, SSP_SKIP_EMBED=true in the MSBuild conditions), and the conditions
  compared against the literal 'true', so the numeric form was silently ignored
  and the recursive publish ran anyway. Normalising here, in a file that both
  the outer build and every nested publish evaluate, makes all truthy spellings
  mean the same thing whether they arrive as an environment variable or as a
  global property. Read SSP_SKIP_EMBED_EFFECTIVE, never the raw variable.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <SSP_SKIP_EMBED_EFFECTIVE Condition="'$(SSP_SKIP_EMBED)' == 'true' OR '$(SSP_SKIP_EMBED)' == '1' OR '$(SSP_SKIP_EMBED)' == 'yes'">true</SSP_SKIP_EMBED_EFFECTIVE>
    <SSP_SKIP_EMBED_EFFECTIVE Condition="'$(SSP_SKIP_EMBED_EFFECTIVE)' == ''">$(SSP_SKIP_EMBED)</SSP_SKIP_EMBED_EFFECTIVE>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
